  </Folder>
  <Folder>
   <name>Shrines</name>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Amanuma Kumano Shrine · 天沼熊野神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Amanuma village, founded by tradition in 768. It was called Jūnisho Gongen until the Meiji era, when it took the name Kumano Jinja.
Enshrined kami: Izanami
Amanuma · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7417.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.626784,35.708911</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Amanuma Hachiman Shrine · 天沼八幡神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of Nakayato in old Amanuma village, said to date from the 1570s or 80s. Ichikishimahime, enshrined here since a local Itsukushima shrine was merged in 1907, is prayed to for safe childbirth, and the Ōtori shrine in its grounds holds a Tori-no-ichi fair in November.
Enshrined kami: Hachiman, Ichikishimahime
Amanuma · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7416.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.623104,35.708231</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Hon-Amanuma Inari Shrine · 本天沼稲荷神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of Honmura in old Amanuma village, founded before 1614. Because Amanuma was land of the Sannō shrine in Akasaka, today's Hie Shrine, its inner sanctuary was brought from there around 1868, and a large votive painting of the Sannō festival is a ward cultural property.
Enshrined kami: Ukemochi
Amanuma · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7536.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.62485,35.712604</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Asagaya Shinmei-gū · 阿佐ヶ谷神明宮</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Asagaya village. Its sacred object is a stone brought back from the Miyagawa River at Ise in the 1190s, and Asagaya-bayashi festival music, handed down since the late Edo period, is played on its Noh stage at the autumn festival.
Enshrined kami: Amaterasu, Tsukuyomi, Susanoo, Toyouke
Asagaya · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7408.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.636988,35.707221</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Mabashi Inari Shrine · 馬橋稲荷神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Mabashi village, said to date from the late Kamakura period. Its stone torii, carved with a rising and a descending dragon, is counted among Tokyo's three dragon torii, and the bell in its vermilion gate is said to be the largest in Tokyo.
Enshrined kami: Ukanomitama (Inari), Ōmatonotsu
Asagaya · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7413.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.642735,35.703274</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Suginami Sarutahiko Shrine · 杉並猿田彦神社</name>
    <description>Its kami was brought from Tsubaki Ōkami Yashiro in Mie, regarded as Sarutahiko's head shrine. As the kami who leads the way, Sarutahiko draws people praying before building work and new ventures, and for business.
Enshrined kami: Sarutahiko
Asagaya · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並猿田彦神社 https://www.sarutahiko-suginami.com/%E5%BE%A1%E7%A5%AD%E7%A5%9E-%E7%94%B1%E7%B7%92</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.643606,35.6987</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Eifuku Inari Shrine · 永福稲荷神社</name>
    <description>Founded in 1530 as guardian of the neighboring temple Eifukuji, with a spirit from Fushimi Inari in Kyoto, and today the guardian shrine of all Eifuku. Its hall was rebuilt in 1878 facing west, to watch over the whole town.
Enshrined kami: Ukanomitama (Inari)
Eifuku / Nishi-Eifuku · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 東京都神社庁 http://www.tokyo-jinjacho.or.jp/suginami/5965</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.64325,35.672444</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Horinouchi Kumano Shrine · 堀ノ内熊野神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Horinouchi village, founded by tradition in 1267 and run by the nearby temple Myōhōji until Meiji. Its zelkova hall of 1857 carries carving said to be the finest of any shrine building in Suginami.
Enshrined kami: Ōyatsuhime, Isotakeru, Tsumatsuhime
Horinouchi / Umezato · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7531.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.652111,35.686333</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ōtori Shrine (Shimo-Igusa) · 大鷲神社（下井草）</name>
    <description>Its sacred object is a stuffed eagle, with a wingspan of over two metres, that villagers caught in a red pine in January 1921 and enshrined so that its death would bring no curse. A Tori-no-ichi fair is held on November's Days of the Rooster, with kumade rakes sold for good business.
Igusa / Iogi / Shimo-Igusa · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7488.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.628876,35.716049</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ichō Inari Shrine · 銀杏稲荷神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Shimo-Igusa village, named after a great ginkgo that once grew on the hill behind it. A ridge plate dates its founding to around 1616, by the Iguchi family who settled here.
Igusa / Iogi / Shimo-Igusa · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7489.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.626633,35.718796</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Izumi Kumano Shrine · 和泉熊野神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Izumi village, founded by tradition in 1267 with a spirit from the Kumano shrines of Kii. The present hall dates from 1863, and Jōmon and Kofun-period pottery has been dug up in the grounds.
Enshrined kami: Amenominakanushi, Izanagi, Izanami
Izumi / Daitabashi side · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7419.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.647758,35.676423</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kifune Shrine (Izumi) · 貴船神社（和泉）</name>
    <description>An outlying shrine of Izumi Kumano, enshrining the rain-bringing dragon kami of Kyoto's Kifune Shrine. Its pond, said never to run dry, was where villagers prayed for rain and is said to have given Izumi, 'spring', its name; it dried up around 1965.
Enshrined kami: Takaokami
Izumi / Daitabashi side · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7420.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.64859,35.677849</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kōenji Hikawa Shrine · 高円寺氷川神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of the Hara district of old Kōenji village, founded by tradition in 1189. Its grounds hold the Kishō Jinja, said to be the only weather shrine in Japan.
Enshrined kami: Susanoo
Kōenji · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7483.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.651298,35.704603</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kishō Jinja (Weather Shrine) · 気象神社</name>
    <description>Said to be Japan's only weather shrine, in the grounds of Kōenji Hikawa Shrine. People pray here for fine weather on wedding and travel days; its ema are shaped like geta, after the game of kicking off a sandal to tell the weather, and its festival falls on 1 June, Japan's Weather Day.
Enshrined kami: Omoikane
Kōenji · As of 2026-09-23
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2015/06/koenji-hikawa.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.65101,35.704718</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kōenji Tenso Shrine · 高円寺天祖神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Kōenji village, founded by its own account in 1087 with a spirit brought from Ise. The worship hall dates from 1831, and a stone water basin in the grounds is carved with the year 1664.
Enshrined kami: Amaterasu
Kōenji · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7470.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.657541,35.700015</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Tanaka Inari Shrine · 田中稲荷神社</name>
    <description>A guardian shrine for the farmers of old Kōenji village, named for its place among the rice paddies along the Momozono River. The road in front is the Horinouchi Shindō, built privately around 1900 as a pilgrim road to Myōhōji temple.
Enshrined kami: Ukemochi
Kōenji · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7471.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.659691,35.700619</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kugayama Inari Shrine · 久我山稲荷神社</name>
    <description>Kugayama's guardian shrine. At its summer festival on 24 July a rare yudate rite is held: water is boiled in a great cauldron, and bamboo leaves dipped in it are shaken over the crowd, a custom that began with prayers to end an epidemic.
Enshrined kami: Ukemochi
Kugayama · As of 2026-09-23
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2015/06/kugayamainari.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.59653,35.68847</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ōmiyamae Kasuga Shrine · 大宮前春日神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Ōmiyamae-shinden village, founded by tradition in the 1650s by Iguchi Hachirōemon, who opened its farmland. A stone reading 'Ōmiyamae chinju' in front of the hall keeps alive a place name that has otherwise disappeared.
Enshrined kami: Takemikazuchi, Futsunushi, Amenokoyane, Himegami (Kasuga)
Miyamae · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7548.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.60936,35.69283</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Narimune Suga Shrine · 成宗須賀神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of the main settlement of old Narimune village. Before Meiji it was called Gozu Tennō-sha, because Susanoo was then identified with the Buddhist deity Gozu Tennō. Its records burned in the 1850s, so its founding date is uncertain.
Enshrined kami: Susanoo
Narita · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7523.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.631616,35.697802</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Osaki Kumano Shrine · 尾﨑熊野神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of Osaki in old Narimune village, founded by tradition in the late Kamakura period by warriors who came from Kamakura. Its sacred black pine, some 400 to 500 years old, is a ward natural monument, and Jōmon pottery and dwellings have been excavated in the grounds.
Enshrined kami: Isotakeru, Tsumatsuhime, Ōyatsuhime
Narita · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7516.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.631288,35.693604</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Narimune Hakusan Shrine · 成宗白山神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of Shirahata in old Narimune village. The name Shirahata, 'white banner', comes from the legend that Minamoto no Yoriyoshi saw a white cloud like a Genji banner here on his way to war in Ōshū.
Enshrined kami: Izanami
Narita · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7517.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.637174,35.686959</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Nishi-Takaido Shōan Inari Shrine · 西高井戸松庵稲荷神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Shōan village, which by tradition a doctor named Shōan opened in the 1650s. Two stone towers at the entrance, dated 1690 and 1693, show the village was there by then.
Enshrined kami: Ukemochi
Nishi-Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7502.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.595917,35.699667</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Fushimi Inari Shrine (Nishi-Ogikubo) · 伏見稲荷神社（西荻窪）</name>
    <description>Founded in 1951 by the shopkeepers of this street, who brought a spirit from Kyoto's Fushimi Inari to pray for good trade; Nishi-Ogi Fushimi-dori is named after it. The pin marks the red torii on the street: the small shrine is about 30m down the alley behind it.
Nishi-Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 西荻伏見通り商店会 https://nishiogi-fushimidori.com/history</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.598915,35.704259</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ogikubo Hachiman Shrine · 荻窪八幡神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Kami-Ogikubo village, founded by tradition in the 890s. Ōta Dōkan is said to have prayed here in 1477 and planted an umbrella pine, the Dōkan-maki, which is now the sacred tree and a ward natural monument. The shrine also keeps a great banner written by Katsu Kaishū.
Enshrined kami: Hachiman
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7458.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.606329,35.710621</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ogikubo Hakusan Shrine · 荻窪白山神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Shimo-Ogikubo village, said to date from the 1470s or 80s. It is known as a shrine for toothache: legend says the kami told a sufferer in a dream to eat with chopsticks cut from the bush clover in the grounds, and the pain went away.
Enshrined kami: Izanami
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7455.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.61605,35.705496</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Minami-Ogikubo Tenso Shrine · 南荻窪天祖神社</name>
    <description>A shrine already stood here at a land survey in 1584. In the Edo period it was called Shinmei-sha, which gave the neighborhood its old name, Shinmei, and to its east there was once a riding ground lined with cherry trees.
Enshrined kami: Amaterasu
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7544.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.609556,35.7005</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Tabata Shrine · 田端神社</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of old Tabata village, once a Tenjin shrine called Kitano Jinja; its name means 'at the edge of the rice fields'. Ōkuninushi, enshrined here, is prayed to for leg and back pain, and the wooden mallets offered in thanks are a registered ward folk cultural property.
Enshrined kami: Sugawara no Michizane (Tenjin), Ōkuninushi
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7447.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.626204,35.693975</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ōmiya Hachimangū · 大宮八幡宮</name>
    <description>Founded by tradition in 1063. On his way to war, Minamoto no Yoriyoshi saw eight bands of white cloud here like Genji banners, and after his victory he brought a spirit from Iwashimizu Hachimangū in Kyoto to found the shrine.
Enshrined kami: Hachiman, Emperor Chūai, Empress Jingū
Ōmiya / Matsunoki · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 大宮八幡宮 https://www.ohmiya-hachimangu.or.jp/hachimangu/history</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.639664,35.682375</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ōmiya Tenmangū · 大宮天満宮</name>
    <description>A shrine to Sugawara no Michizane, patron of learning, within the grounds of Ōmiya Hachimangū. Students come here to pray for exam success.
Enshrined kami: Sugawara no Michizane (Tenjin)
Ōmiya / Matsunoki · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 大宮八幡宮 https://www.ohmiya-hachimangu.or.jp/hachimangu</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.639573,35.682639</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Nakase Tenso Shrine · 中瀬天祖神社</name>
    <description>It began as a hall to the Ten Rasetsu on the land of Myōshōji temple, and became a Shinto shrine when Shinto and Buddhism were separated in 1868. Its sacred object is a stone held to be powerful, and it is known locally as a shrine for having children.
Enshrined kami: Amaterasu, Ichikishimahime, Ukemochi
Shimizu · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7485.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.617333,35.718556</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Shimo-Takaido Hachiman Shrine · 下高井戸八幡神社（浜田山八幡）</name>
    <description>By tradition founded in 1457, when Ōta Dōkan, while building Edo Castle, prayed here for the work to go safely and brought a spirit from Tsurugaoka Hachimangū in Kamakura.
Enshrined kami: Hachiman
Shimo-Takaido / Sakurajōsui side · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 東京都神社庁 http://www.tokyo-jinjacho.or.jp/suginami/5404</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.63042,35.67522</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kami-Takaido Dairokuten Shrine · 上高井戸第六天神社</name>
    <description>Its stone guardian lions, dated 1771, are said to be the oldest outdoor pair in Suginami, and its main hall, rebuilt in 1856, is richly carved. In 1972 the buildings were moved slightly north for the Chūō Expressway, whose elevated road now runs right past the torii.
Enshrined kami: Omodaru, Kashikone
Takaido / Fujimigaoka · As of 2026-09-23
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2015/06/dairokutenjinja.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.61217,35.67903</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kashinomiya Inari Shrine · 柏の宮稲荷神社</name>
    <description>Built in the Meiji era as the private shrine of Kashinomiya-en, the garden estate that is now Kashinomiya Park, and moved to its present spot when the park was laid out. It sits at the top of a flight of steps, guarded by stone foxes on a base dated 1857.
Enshrined kami: Ukanomitama (Inari), Ukemochi
Takaido / Fujimigaoka · As of 2026-09-23
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2023/09/kashinomiyainari.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.62485,35.67881</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Igusa Hachimangū · 井草八幡宮</name>
    <description>The guardian shrine of the old villages of Kami-Igusa and Shimo-Igusa. Until the Meiji era it was also called Osonoi Hachimangū, after the area's old name.
Enshrined kami: Hachiman
Zenpukuji · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7503.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.595583,35.716083</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ichikishima Shrine (Zenpukuji Benzaiten) · 市杵島神社（善福寺弁天）</name>
    <description>The Benzaiten shrine of Zenpukuji Pond, founded by legend in 1197 with a spirit brought from Enoshima. As guardian of the pond's spring it drew rain-prayer processions in droughts, and villages as far off as Nerima and Nakano helped pay for its rebuilding in 1840.
Enshrined kami: Ichikishimahime
Zenpukuji · As of 2026-09-23
Source: 杉並区公式ホームページ https://www.city.suginami.tokyo.jp/s113/7507.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.588074,35.716306</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
