  <Folder>
   <name>Temples</name>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kamadera (Tōunji) · 釜寺（東運寺）</name>
    <description>A Jōdo temple said to date from 1573, with an iron rice cauldron on its roof. The story comes from the legend of Anju and Zushiō: when Zushiō was about to be boiled in a cauldron, the Jizō he carried took his place.
Hōnan · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/10/post-188.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.657776,35.680794</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
  </Folder>
  <Folder>
   <name>Culture</name>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Suginami Local History Museum Annex · 杉並区立郷土博物館分館</name>
    <description>The free annex of the ward's history museum, opened in 2007 in Amanuma Benten-ike Park, in buildings a golf company once used.
Amanuma · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2018/07/kyoudo-hakubutukan.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.621887,35.70821</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Laputa Asagaya · ラピュタ阿佐ヶ谷</name>
    <description>A small cinema for Japanese films, mostly from the 1950s and 60s, shown in month-long seasons, many never released on DVD. It opened in 1998 for animation and is named after the flying island in Gulliver's Travels.
Asagaya · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2015/09/laputa.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.635361,35.705544</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Gundam statue · ガンダム像</name>
    <description>A bronze Gundam reaching for the sky, put up outside Kami-Igusa station in 2008 because Sunrise, the studio behind Mobile Suit Gundam, is nearby. The trains leave to the Gundam theme.
Kami-Igusa · As of 2026-09-25
Source: 上井草商店街 http://www.kami-igusa.com/area/animation</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.603745,35.725014</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Za-Kōenji · 座・高円寺</name>
    <description>The ward's theatre, a tent-like building by Itō Toyō opened in 2009, with three halls, an archive of plays and a café with picture books.
Kōenji · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/03/za-koenji.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.654068,35.705627</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>St Margaret's Chapel · 立教女学院 聖マーガレット礼拝堂</name>
    <description>A Romanesque chapel from 1932 by J. V. W. Bergamini, architect of St Luke's Hospital, at Rikkyo Jogakuin. It's concrete but warm with North American oak inside. It stands on school grounds, so check when visitors can come.
Kugayama · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/04/st-margarets-college-schools.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.590164,35.694756</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Seikō Lodging · 西郊ロッヂング</name>
    <description>A boarding house that moved here from Hongō in 1931, with Western-style rooms that locked, rare at the time. The 1931 main building and 1938 annex are registered tangible cultural properties. It is now an inn and apartments, so admire it from the street.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/11/seiko-lodging.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.625565,35.702972</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Gengi Sanbō (Suginami Poetry House) · 幻戯山房（すぎなみ詩歌館）</name>
    <description>The 1955 house of Kadokawa Gen'yoshi, poet and founder of the Kadokawa publishing house. It was given to the ward and has been open to the public since 2009. His study shows things from his life, and every room looks onto the garden.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/03/gengisanbo.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.624741,35.698811</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Katsura Bunko · かつら文庫</name>
    <description>In 1958 the children's author Ishii Momoko opened part of her home as a small library for the neighborhood's children. It still runs as a branch of the Tokyo Children's Library, and adults are welcome too.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/09/katsura-bunko.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.625153,35.702042</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Ōtaguro Park Memorial Hall · 大田黒公園記念館</name>
    <description>The brick-colored studio that the music critic Ōtaguro Motoo built in 1933, now in the Japanese garden of Ōtaguro Park. His German piano and gramophone are still there.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/03/otaguropark-kinenkan.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.624161,35.701576</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Tekigaisō · 荻外荘</name>
    <description>The house of Konoe Fumimaro, three times prime minister, designed by Itō Chūta in 1927. It is a national historic site. The ward restored it over ten years and opened it as Tekigaisō Park in December 2024.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2025/03/tekigaiso.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.622894,35.699215</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Tekigaisō Exhibition Building · 荻外荘展示棟</name>
    <description>Kuma Kengo's exhibition building across the lane from Tekigaisō, opened in July 2025, with a café and shop downstairs and exhibitions upstairs.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2025/09/tekigaiso-tenjito.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.622803,35.698929</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Suginami Animation Museum · 杉並アニメーションミュージアム</name>
    <description>A free museum of Japanese animation on the third floor of Suginami Kaikan: its history, and how anime is made. Closed on Mondays.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: 杉並アニメーションミュージアム https://sam.or.jp/</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.607574,35.710491</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Suginami Local History Museum · 杉並区立郷土博物館</name>
    <description>The ward's history museum, opened in 1989 near the Zenpukuji River; you go in through a thatched gatehouse. The land belonged to the Saga family, and in 1937 their daughter Hiro left from here to marry Pujie, brother of the last emperor of China.
Ōmiya / Matsunoki · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2018/02/suginami-historical-museum.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.644547,35.685661</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Takachiho Budōjō · 高千穂学園武道場</name>
    <description>Built in 1913 as the judo and kendo hall of Takachiho College, it's the oldest wooden school building in the ward and a ward cultural property. The original 36 springs and 10 large jars are still under the floor.
Ōmiya / Matsunoki · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2016/07/takachiho-budojo.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.638245,35.682804</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Misawa Bauhaus Collection · ミサワバウハウスコレクション</name>
    <description>Japan's only museum of the Bauhaus, opened by Misawa Homes in 1996 at its research institute. It holds about 1,500 works and puts on free exhibitions, so check the dates before you go.
Takaido / Fujimigaoka · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/12/misawa-bauhaus.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.614365,35.679562</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Risshō Kōsei-kai Great Sacred Hall · 立正佼成会 大聖堂</name>
