    <description>A cake shop on Itsukaichi Kaidō that began about 70 years ago as a dried-goods store. The favorite is the Indian pudding, a steamed, liqueur-scented pudding topped with melon and cream.
Narita · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/10/koumiya.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.639893,35.693363</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Miharadō · 三原堂</name>
    <description>A wagashi shop opened in 1935 under a name shared by Miharadō of Yotsuya. Known for thin, crisp shio senbei with the rice grains left in.
Nishi-Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/10/miharado.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.600586,35.704487</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kidaya · 喜田屋</name>
    <description>A wagashi shop opened in 1948, famous for mame daifuku packed with red peas under a thin skin. In winter they can sell out before noon.
Nishi-Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/10/kidaya.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.60083,35.705513</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Seirindō Shobō · 盛林堂書房</name>
    <description>A secondhand bookshop founded in 1949 and on this spot since the year after, strong in mysteries and literary fiction. Ibuse Masuji and Matsumoto Seichō were customers.
Nishi-Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2019/03/seirindou-syobou.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.599808,35.702263</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Niwatori Bunko · にわとり文庫</name>
    <description>A secondhand bookshop opened in 2005, a Year of the Rooster, hence the name 'Chicken Library'. Kokeshi dolls line the front, and old toys sit among the books.
Nishi-Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2022/10/niwatoribunko.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.602173,35.703732</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Kameya · 御菓子司 亀屋</name>
    <description>A wagashi shop that moved here from Yotsuya in 1930, after the Great Kantō Earthquake. Its best-known sweet is the café-au-lait daifuku: coffee-flavored bean paste and cream, sold frozen.
Ogikubo · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/10/kameya.html</description>
    <Point>
     <coordinates>139.616364,35.707138</coordinates>
    </Point>
   </Placemark>
   <Placemark>
    <name>Koganedō · こがね堂</name>
    <description>Rice crackers made by hand, from pounding the mochi onwards, in front of Takaido station since 1947. The hina-arare for the Dolls' Festival sell out every year.
Takaido / Fujimigaoka · As of 2026-09-25
Source: すぎなみ学倶楽部 https://www.suginamigaku.org/2014/10/koganedo.html</description>
